course: physics
lesson: 25
title: Keeping warm and keeping cool: insulation and sweat
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A woolly jumper does not make any heat; your body makes it, and the jumper traps still air between its fibres, which passes heat very badly, so the heat stays in.
explain: Insulation slows heat moving either way, so the same coat that keeps you warm would keep a snowman cold, and the same flask keeps tea hot and juice cold all day.
explain: To cool down, bodies use a trick: when water dries off the skin it takes heat with it, so sweat cools you, and stepping out of the pool on a warm day makes you shiver.
explain: Dogs hardly sweat, so they pant to dry their wet tongues, and elephants flap their huge thin ears so the blood inside gives its heat to the air.
explain: Sunlight pours into a parked car through the glass, but the warmth from the seats cannot get out easily, so the car gets dangerously hot inside, far hotter than the air outside.
fact: Wool, fur and fluffy materials keep heat in by trapping still air, which passes heat very badly.
fact: Insulation slows heat flow in both directions, so a coat wrapped round a snowman makes it last longer.
fact: A polar bear stays warm in the Arctic with thick fur and a layer of fat, and its skin under the pale fur is black.
fact: A flask has two walls with almost no air between them, so heat can hardly cross, and shiny walls bounce heat rays back.
fact: Thick walls, fluffy loft insulation and double windows slow heat leaking out of a house, so it needs much less fuel to keep warm.
fact: Sweat cools you because as it evaporates it takes heat from your skin; on a very humid day it cannot evaporate well, and you feel hot and sticky.
fact: You feel cold getting out of a pool, even on a warm day, because the water on your skin evaporates and carries heat away, and wind makes it faster.
fact: Dogs hardly sweat, so they cool down by panting, which dries their wet tongue and mouth.
fact: A car parked in the Sun gets much hotter inside than outside, and a dog must never be left in one, because it gets dangerously hot in minutes.
fact: A greenhouse stays warm in the same way as a parked car: light gets in easily, but the warmth cannot escape well.
fact: An elephant's big thin ears are full of blood vessels and give off heat like radiators when it flaps them.
fact: Desert foxes have big ears that help them lose heat, while Arctic foxes have small ears that keep heat in.
exercise: How does a woolly jumper keep you warm? | It traps still air that keeps your body heat in | It makes heat from the wool | It soaks up heat from the room | It blocks out the cold rays that come from outside
exercise: Why does a snowman last longer wrapped in a coat? | The coat keeps the warm air away from the snow | The coat makes cold of its own | The coat makes the snow heavier | The coat stops the snowman from moving in the wind
exercise: How does a polar bear stay warm in the Arctic? | Thick fur and a layer of fat keep its body heat in | It has a heater in its chest | It never goes outside | It drinks warm water every day to heat up its insides
exercise: What colour is a polar bear's skin under its pale fur? | Black | White | Pink | Yellow
exercise: How does a flask keep tea hot all day? | Its walls have almost no air between them, so heat can hardly cross | It has a tiny heater inside | The lid squeezes the heat into the tea | Its metal makes heat of its own
exercise: Can the same flask keep juice cold all day? | Yes, it slows heat moving in as well as out | No, a flask can only ever work for hot drinks | Only if you add some salt to the juice first | Only if the outside of the flask is painted white
exercise: Why does a house have a loft full of fluffy stuff and double windows? | To slow heat leaking out in winter | To keep the mice in the loft warm and cosy | To make the house quieter, and that is all | To stop the house from moving in the wind
exercise: Why do you sweat when you run? | Sweat drying off your skin takes heat away and cools you | Sweat makes your legs move faster | Sweat keeps your skin warm | Your body is getting rid of extra water it cannot use any more
exercise: Why do you feel cold getting out of a pool on a warm day? | Water drying off your skin carries heat away | The pool water was full of ice | Your blood stops flowing for a while after swimming | The Sun stops shining on wet people
exercise: How does a dog cool down on a hot day? | It pants, drying its wet tongue to cool itself | It sweats all over its fur, just like people do | It drinks hot water to warm up and then cool down | It rolls about in the hot sand to dry out its fur
exercise: Why must a dog never be left in a parked car on a sunny day? | The car gets dangerously hot inside in minutes | The dog will steal all the sweets left on the seat | The dog will learn to open the windows and escape | Dogs get bored and chew up the seats of the car
exercise: Why does a car in the Sun get much hotter inside than outside? | Sunlight gets in, but the warmth from the seats cannot easily get out | The engine keeps running by itself | The seats make their own heat | The air outside is cooler because of the wind
exercise: Why do elephants flap their huge ears? | Blood in the thin ears gives off heat to the air | To chase away every lion that comes near the herd | To hear far-off sounds better | To make wind for their babies
exercise: Why do Arctic foxes have small ears? | Small ears lose less heat in the cold | Small ears grow faster in a cold climate | Small ears are harder for wolves to bite | Small ears keep the snow out, and nothing more
exercise: Why do you feel hot and sticky on a very humid day? | Sweat cannot evaporate well, so it cannot cool you | Your body makes no sweat at all when the air is humid | The humid air is full of heat rays | Humid air makes you heavier
exercise: How is a greenhouse like a car parked in the Sun? | Light gets in easily, but the warmth cannot escape well | Both have engines inside | Both are cooled by plants | Both are painted black
test: Mum says "put a jumper on, it'll warm you up!" What is the more exact truth? | The jumper keeps in heat your body makes; it makes none itself | The jumper makes heat from the wool | The jumper soaks heat from the air into you | The jumper only works when it is new
test: A friend wants to keep his ice lolly frozen on a hot walk. Which would help most? | Wrap it in a thick woolly scarf | Hold it tightly in his bare hand | Put it in a black plastic bag in the Sun | Wave it about in the warm air
test: What would happen to a polar bear if its fur got soaked and flat? | It would lose heat faster, because the trapped air was gone | It would warm up faster | Nothing at all would change | It would turn white all over
test: A dog is panting hard in the back of a hot car with the windows shut. What should you do? | Get an adult to help at once, because it is an emergency | Leave it, dogs like warm cars | Give it a jumper to cover up | Wait about an hour and then check whether the dog has cooled down
test: why'd I get goosebumps getting out the pool when it's bloody 28 degrees out? | Water drying off your skin is pulling heat away | The pool water is secretly frozen | Your skin forgets how to be warm after swimming | Hot days make you colder inside
test: In a cartoon a snowman melts faster because someone puts a coat on him "to keep him cosy". What is wrong? | A coat would slow the melting, not speed it up | Nothing, coats always warm up whatever is inside them | Coats make snow turn into steam | The coat would make him grow
test: A flask of hot tea and a flask of iced juice sit side by side all day. What is clever about the flask? | It simply slows heat in either direction, not knowing hot from cold | It knows which drink is hot and which is cold | It only works on one of them | It turns the juice warm and the tea cold
test: Why might a desert fox have huge ears while an Arctic fox has tiny ones? | Big ears lose heat in the heat, small ears save heat in the cold | Desert foxes need to hear camels | Arctic foxes have no use for ears | Big ears grow only in sand
test: Two identical houses are heated in winter, but one has fluffy loft insulation and double windows. Which needs less fuel? | The insulated house | The house without insulation | Both need exactly the same | Neither needs any fuel at all
test: A runner on a humid, still day overheats far more than on a dry, breezy day at the same temperature. Why? | Sweat dries badly in humid, still air, so it cools her less | Humid air is always hotter | Breezes make the Sun's rays much weaker, so they heat her less | Dry air makes runners slower
test: You put a wet cloth round a bottle of water and leave it in the breeze. What happens to the water? | It gets cooler as the cloth dries | It gets warmer as the cloth dries | It freezes solid in minutes | Nothing changes at all
exam: Identify the principal means by which a woollen garment maintains the warmth of the wearer. | The retention of still air, which is a poor conductor of heat | The generation of heat by the fibres | The absorption of heat from the surroundings | The reflection of the cold of the air away from the body of the wearer
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cooling effect of perspiration? | Evaporation of moisture from the skin removes heat from the body | Perspiration is intrinsically of low temperature when it is produced | Perspiration reflects the rays of the Sun | Perspiration seals heat within the skin
exam: It is given that an animal is to be left in a closed vehicle exposed to the Sun. Ascertain the hazard thereby presented. | The interior temperature rises rapidly to a dangerous level | The interior remains at the external temperature | The interior cools progressively | No hazard of any description is presented to the animal within
exam: State the reason for which a vacuum flask preserves the temperature of its contents in either direction. | The near absence of air between its walls impedes the passage of heat | It contains a heating element | It distinguishes hot contents from cold | It generates cold within its walls
